1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,020
Hello, everyone, and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:05,680
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:10,320
My name is Katrin, and today we've got some kids for you in this dialogue.

4
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,040
They're doing something that's very common in America.

5
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:16,260
I'm not so sure if it's common elsewhere, but we'll find that out later.

6
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,820
So today we're going to ask for money because we are having a fundraiser.

7
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:23,700
So this is a very interesting word.

8
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,320
Why don't we take a look at it in Vocabulary Preview?

9
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:31,010
So a fundraiser is a thing.

10
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:31,750
It's an event.

11
00:00:32,070 --> 00:00:38,830
It's something that you do at a school or a business to try and collect money for a special event or charity.

12
00:00:39,310 --> 00:00:39,710
That's right.

13
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:44,640
So money, you can also say is funds, right?

14
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:45,760
And that's what you're trying to do.

15
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:46,630
You're trying to collect.

16
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:54,640
You're trying to gather a lot of money or funds in order for, like you say, some sort of objective purpose.

17
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:55,090
Right.

18
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:59,920
So it's not just like we're having a fundraiser to earn more profits this year.

19
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:03,270
It's usually an event that's for some kind of charity or good cause.

20
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:13,330
So for example, the company is having a fundraiser for Jenny's child because he's sick and they can't pay for medical care.

21
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,040
So a fundraiser is for a good cause.

22
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:17,040
Exactly.

23
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:18,590
So that's what this dialogue is all about today.

24
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,570
Why don't we listen to the dialogue for the first time and we'll be back in a bit.

25
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:31,310
Okay, Mark, it's your turn to ring the doorbell.

26
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:32,550
I did it last time.

27
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:35,930
I hate going door to door and I hate asking for money.

28
00:01:36,310 --> 00:01:40,650
But we need to raise enough money for the school fundraiser so that our class can win the pizza party.

29
00:01:41,210 --> 00:01:43,170
You do want to have a pizza party, don't you?

30
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:44,950
Yes, but...

31
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:45,790
Just go already.

32
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:48,710
No one's coming.

33
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:49,590
Try again.

34
00:01:50,190 --> 00:01:51,230
Maybe there's no one home.

35
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:52,830
Of course there's someone home.

36
00:01:53,190 --> 00:01:56,030
There are two cars in the driveway and I can see lights on in the house.

37
00:01:57,460 --> 00:01:57,960
Hello?

38
00:01:58,640 --> 00:01:59,180
Anybody home?

39
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:02,740
We would like to know if you want to sponsor us in our school fundraiser.

40
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:06,200
50% of the profits go towards the new school playground.

41
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:10,070
I don't know why anyone would want what's in this catalogue anyway.

42
00:02:10,470 --> 00:02:12,450
It's just a bunch of tacky Christmas ornaments.

43
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:19,120
CDs of old people singing Christmas songs and special crackers and cheeses and boxes of chocolate.

44
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:19,690
You don't like chocolate?

45
00:02:20,310 --> 00:02:20,990
Not this kind.

46
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:24,480
They've got weird names like ganache and praline.

47
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,880
Look, I just saw someone walking around inside.

48
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:29,130
These people are being very rude.

49
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:31,430
Finally, someone's coming.

50
00:02:31,990 --> 00:02:33,330
They don't look too happy.

51
00:02:33,810 --> 00:02:34,370
Hi, sir.

52
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:37,010
Would you like to sponsor us or make a donation to...

53
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:38,330
What grade are you kids in?

54
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Grade 7.

55
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,880
Then for goodness sake, don't you see this sign?

56
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:43,310
Can't you read?

57
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:44,880
No soliciting.

58
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:55,760
Alright, so obviously the kids are selling candy and a lot of different things for their new playground.

59
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:56,680
That's the fundraiser, right?

60
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,560
So they use a lot of interesting words and phrases there.

61
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:06,440
Why don't we take a look at a couple of them on Language Takeaway.

62
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:07,040
Language Takeaway.

63
00:03:09,030 --> 00:03:13,470
Well, the first word is one that Marco, you've already talked a little bit about today.

64
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:16,110
We talked about fundraiser, to raise.

65
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:19,830
Well, we do have the verb here, to raise enough money.

66
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:26,050
So raise is a verb that means to collect or gather or to get.

67
00:03:26,390 --> 00:03:26,910
That's right.

68
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:31,150
So you wouldn't really say we are collecting money or we are gathering money.

69
00:03:31,570 --> 00:03:36,110
You say we are raising money for the victims of the earthquake in Haiti.

70
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:37,040
That's right.

71
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:41,600
And the reason we don't say we are earning money is because you're not doing any work.

72
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:41,800
Right.

73
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:46,080
Really, you're not going to the job at 9 o'clock and leaving the job at 5 o'clock.

74
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:47,800
You're actually asking people for donations.

75
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:48,680
Exactly.

76
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:50,000
So that's what you're doing.

77
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:51,660
You are raising money.

78
00:03:52,760 --> 00:04:00,930
And now moving on, another way that you can help people maybe through a charity or donation is also to sponsor.

79
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:03,760
To sponsor someone or something.

80
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:08,890
Okay, so people can sponsor events or people and companies can also do this as well.

81
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:15,560
So to sponsor someone is basically to give them money in support of a cause.

82
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:19,320
For example, have you ever done one of those 5K runs or 10K runs?

83
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:26,380
Did you have to collect sponsors basically to find people who would help pay money for you to do this run?

84
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:44,040
Not personally, but I know people who have, who actually get companies interested and maybe that person, for example, is going to run 5K in this race in order to raise money for victims of some serious illness.

85
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:48,760
And so they get sponsors, they get companies to donate money for this cause.

86
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:49,500
Right.

87
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:57,860
You could have a person or a company sponsor you, they could be your sponsor by giving you money that you can then use for this charity or special cause.

88
00:04:58,080 --> 00:04:58,200
That's right.

89
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:09,800
So the difference between a fundraiser and to sponsor something or someone is that sponsoring is not always for a charity or for some sort of non-profit cause, right?

90
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:13,840
We have many sponsors for concerts or for sporting events.

91
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:13,970
That's right.

92
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:22,490
So oftentimes when you see athletes, they have their sponsors or their sponsors logos written all over their jerseys, the clothes that they wear.

93
00:05:22,710 --> 00:05:30,810
So for example, if you're watching Formula One racing, you see the Ferrari and the bank emblems and all these different things.

94
00:05:31,110 --> 00:05:31,490
That's right.

95
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:35,290
So those are different ways of sponsoring events or people.

96
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:44,120
And now moving on, we have a word that's not really related to money and fundraisers, but it's a very interesting word and very common.

97
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:49,640
When the kids mention that they're selling a bunch of tacky Christmas ornaments.

98
00:05:49,820 --> 00:05:50,280
Tacky.

99
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:51,640
Tacky.

100
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:57,840
So we're going to hear this a lot and the interesting thing about the word tacky is that it means something different to everyone.

101
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:05,460
So what I think is tacky might be just normal for Marco and what Marco thinks is really tacky might be something that I actually like.

102
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:09,020
And so tacky is basically a bad thing.

103
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:14,040
It means something is not good looking or not very fashionable.

104
00:06:16,390 --> 00:06:17,390
It's like in bad taste.

105
00:06:18,030 --> 00:06:21,050
But as you say, taste is something subjective, right?

106
00:06:21,350 --> 00:06:28,910
So maybe for example, sometimes we say that when girls wear too much makeup, it's very tacky, bright red lipstick and blue eyeshadow.

107
00:06:29,090 --> 00:06:29,250
Exactly.

108
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:32,560
So a guy might say, oh, that's so tacky.

109
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:35,480
Whereas another person might not think it's tacky at all.

110
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:45,160
So this happens a lot and we use this word a lot with decorations, for example, for someone's house, you may describe it as tacky or a restaurant or a bar.

111
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:46,120
Right.

112
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:56,100
So for maybe sometimes those Italian restaurants that have the red and white checkered table claws and the pictures of the Italians with the mustaches, I might think that's a little bit tacky.

113
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:58,380
But maybe you think it's cute.

114
00:06:59,040 --> 00:06:59,240
Right.

115
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:01,520
So something that's tacky.

116
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:04,100
And as we say, people use it all the time.

117
00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:05,780
And it's not necessarily a bad thing.

118
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:11,480
You can just say, oh, it's a little bit tacky or I think this thing is a little bit tacky.

119
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:12,480
Okay.

120
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:13,640
And that's it, right?

121
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:14,420
Solicit.

122
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:16,240
Solicit.

123
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:17,240
Okay.

124
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:24,760
So a little bit of a language takeaway, towards the end of the dialogue, when the kids were reading the sign, the sign said, no soliciting.

125
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:26,120
And they didn't understand what that means.

126
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:29,160
What does it mean when you have a sign that says no soliciting?

127
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:29,840
Okay.

128
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:35,180
So to solicit is to try to sell something or to offer a service.

129
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:40,840
So basically the sign means, please don't come to my house and sell me stuff.

130
00:07:40,840 --> 00:07:46,200
For example, we have door to door insurance salesmen or makeup salespeople.

131
00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:49,420
And these people go to your door and they try to sell you their product.

132
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:53,160
And no soliciting means, don't come to my house.

133
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:55,220
I'm not going to answer the door if you're trying to sell something.

134
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:58,000
So to solicit is to try and sell something.

135
00:07:58,600 --> 00:07:59,600
Okay.

136
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:03,040
But usually in person, trying to make a sale uninvited, right?

137
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:08,340
Because if you're a businessman and you have a meeting, a sales meeting, you're not really soliciting with that other person.

138
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:12,040
No, this is basically trying to sell something to a stranger.

139
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:13,680
Exactly.

140
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:14,680
Okay.

141
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:15,680
Very good.

142
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:16,680
So a lot of interesting words there.

143
