The kids say, we're in grade seven.

240
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:15,490
And the person at the door says, then for goodness sake, don't you see this sign?

241
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,550
Right, for goodness sake.

242
00:13:17,790 --> 00:13:23,230
So this is an expression that's used to indicate maybe you're a little bit frustrated.

243
00:13:23,690 --> 00:13:23,850
Yes.

244
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:25,720
Right.

245
00:13:25,720 --> 00:13:29,000
So it's like, come on, or my goodness, or for Pete's sake.

246
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,920
So we have many ways of saying, all right, come on, I'm a little tired of this.

247
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:34,920
Right.

248
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:35,130
You're irritating me.

249
00:13:35,920 --> 00:13:36,920
Right.

250
00:13:36,920 --> 00:13:40,120
So basically the person's saying, you are too old for this.

251
00:13:40,120 --> 00:13:40,820
You're too old for this.

252
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:42,440
You should read what it says on my door.

253
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:43,380
No soliciting.

254
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:44,800
Right.

255
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:45,800
And so the person's angry.

256
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:46,800
Right.

257
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:52,500
Because these kids are trying to sell her something when clearly she has a sign that says no soliciting.

258
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:52,960
That's right.

259
00:13:53,840 --> 00:13:54,840
Okay.

260
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:55,400
A lot of interesting phrases there.

261
00:13:55,840 --> 00:14:00,700
Why don't we go back, listen to our dialogue for the very last time, and we'll be back to talk with you a little bit more.

262
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:10,480
Okay, Mark, it's your turn to ring the doorbell.

263
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:11,350
I did it last time.

264
00:14:11,630 --> 00:14:14,770
I hate going door to door and I hate asking for money.

265
00:14:15,090 --> 00:14:19,430
But we need to raise enough money for the school fundraiser so that our class can win the pizza party.

266
00:14:20,050 --> 00:14:21,970
You do want to have a pizza party, don't you?

267
00:14:22,310 --> 00:14:23,730
Yes, but...

268
00:14:23,730 --> 00:14:24,590
Just go already.

269
00:14:26,540 --> 00:14:27,500
No one's coming.

270
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:28,400
Try again.

271
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:30,020
Maybe there's no one home.

272
00:14:30,540 --> 00:14:31,600
Of course there's someone home.

273
00:14:31,980 --> 00:14:34,840
There are two cars in the driveway and I can see lights on in the house.

274
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:36,720
Hello?

275
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:37,980
Anybody home?

276
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:41,540
We would like to know if you want to sponsor us in our school fundraiser.

277
00:14:42,400 --> 00:14:45,000
50% of the profits go towards the new school playground.

278
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:48,900
I don't know why anyone would want what's in this catalogue anyway.

279
00:14:49,260 --> 00:14:57,580
It's just a bunch of tacky Christmas ornaments, CDs of old people singing Christmas songs, and special crackers and cheeses and boxes of chocolates.

280
00:14:57,740 --> 00:14:58,460
You don't like chocolate?

281
00:14:59,100 --> 00:14:59,820
Not this kind.

282
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:03,280
They've got weird names like ganache and praline.

283
00:15:03,780 --> 00:15:05,920
Look, I just saw someone walking around inside.

284
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:07,920
These people are being very rude.

285
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:10,260
Finally, someone's coming.

286
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:12,100
They don't look too happy.

287
00:15:12,640 --> 00:15:13,180
Hi, sir.

288
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:15,800
Would you like to sponsor us or make a donation to...

289
00:15:15,960 --> 00:15:17,140
What grade are you kids in?

290
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:18,520
Grade 7.

291
00:15:18,580 --> 00:15:20,880
Then for goodness sake, don't you see this sign?

292
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:22,120
Can't you read?

293
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:23,680
No soliciting?

294
00:15:31,380 --> 00:15:33,060
All right, so door-to-door salesman.

295
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:37,080
I think this used to be a very American thing.

296
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:42,440
I think back in the 50s, you would always see movies where this was actually a profession.

297
00:15:42,440 --> 00:15:42,580
That's right.

298
00:15:43,440 --> 00:15:50,260
People would go door-to-door selling hairbrushes or magazine subscriptions, pretty much everything.

299
00:15:51,060 --> 00:15:52,460
Yeah, you still see it sometimes.

300
00:15:53,000 --> 00:16:02,140
Some people sell insurance or there are a lot of religious door-to-door salespeople who want to sell religious books or magazines.

301
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:08,360
But I think it was much more popular in the 1950s because people didn't really use their phones as much to sell things.

302
00:16:08,940 --> 00:16:13,060
It was cheaper to have someone drive his car to your neighborhood and then go to every single house.

303
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:16,720
But yeah, it's a very interesting job.

304
00:16:16,720 --> 00:16:18,240
I think it'd be very lonely.

305
00:16:18,240 --> 00:16:22,960
And also, I think, well, now maybe that's been reduced a lot because now you have the internet.

306
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:29,520
Oh yeah, well the internet means that the salespeople come to you without you having to get up.

307
00:16:29,880 --> 00:16:34,400
So basically, you're sitting at home in your pajamas and you can order a TV and some clothes.

308
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:39,060
But it's very interesting because I remember you would have all these door-to-door salesmen.

309
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:43,260
For example, I had one visit me once who was selling me encyclopedias.

310
00:16:43,740 --> 00:16:43,980
Really?

311
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:48,000
A big collection of like, I don't know, 37 books or more.

312
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,640
It was a huge amount of books, encyclopedias.

313
00:16:50,640 --> 00:16:51,800
And I don't know if you see that anymore.

314
00:16:52,160 --> 00:16:54,000
Not really, not in the States.

315
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,260
I don't know if anyone buys physical, huge encyclopedias to have in their home.

316
00:16:58,480 --> 00:16:59,760
No, only collectors perhaps.

317
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:01,320
Maybe.

318
00:17:01,320 --> 00:17:02,040
So it's a very interesting profession.

319
00:17:02,500 --> 00:17:05,740
I don't know, in the United States, as you say, it's not very common anymore.

320
00:17:06,120 --> 00:17:08,120
Very little, maybe specific services.

321
00:17:08,760 --> 00:17:09,300
But this is interesting.

322
00:17:09,300 --> 00:17:11,580
Maybe in your country, this is still a big thing.

323
00:17:12,040 --> 00:17:12,540
Yeah, let us know.

324
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:16,600
In your country, are door-to-door salespeople still common?

325
00:17:16,720 --> 00:17:17,200
Do you see them?

326
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:19,200
Our website is EnglishPod.com.

327
00:17:19,220 --> 00:17:19,880
We hope to see you there.

328
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:21,200
All right, bye-bye.

329
00:17:21,200 --> 00:17:28,720
Bye-bye.
